1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,825
Good morning everyone.

2
00:00:00,825 --> 00:00:07,120
In today's seminar, Grant Freeman, a biologist who specialises in identifying insects,

3
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:11,757
and who works for the Australian Quarantine Service, has come to talk to us

4
00:00:11,757 --> 00:00:13,413
about his current research work.

5
00:00:13,413 --> 00:00:15,400
Right, well, over to you, Grant.

6
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:16,625
Good morning, everyone.

7
00:00:16,625 --> 00:00:22,342
I'm sure that you know that the quarantine service regulates all food brought into

8
00:00:22,342 --> 00:00:22,750
Australia.

9
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:28,438
Well, obviously they want to protect Australia from diseases that might come in with

10
00:00:28,438 --> 00:00:29,250
imported goods,

11
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:33,833
but they also want to prevent insect pests from being introduced into the country,

12
00:00:33,833 --> 00:00:36,780
and that's where I have a part to play.

13
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:43,088
Anyway, my current research involves trying to find a particular type of bee, the

14
00:00:43,088 --> 00:00:44,440
Asian Honey Bee,

15
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:49,172
and finding out whether there are any of them around in various states of

16
00:00:49,172 --> 00:00:49,510
Australia.

17
00:00:49,510 --> 00:00:54,250
We discovered a few of them in Queensland once and eradicated them.

18
00:00:54,250 --> 00:01:00,770
Now, we're pretty keen to make sure that there aren't any more getting in, particularly to New South Wales

19
00:01:00,770 --> 00:01:01,800
and other states.

20
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:04,177
What's wrong with Asian Honey Bees?

21
00:01:04,177 --> 00:01:06,950
Are they so different from Australian bees?

22
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:10,326
Well, in fact, they look almost the same,

23
00:01:10,326 --> 00:01:15,390
but they are infested with mites- microscopic creatures which live on them,

24
00:01:15,390 --> 00:01:19,008
and which can seriously damage our own home-grown bees,

25
00:01:19,008 --> 00:01:21,420
or could even wipe them out.

26
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:24,790
Well, what would happen if Australian bees died out?

27
00:01:24,790 --> 00:01:30,125
Well, the honey from Australian bees is of excellent quality, much better than the

28
00:01:30,125 --> 00:01:32,030
stuff the Asian bees produce.

29
00:01:32,030 --> 00:01:36,921
In fact, Australia exports native Queen bees to a large number of countries

30
00:01:36,921 --> 00:01:38,050
because of this.

31
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:43,576
When the European Honey Bee was first discovered out in the bush, we found

32
00:01:43,576 --> 00:01:45,550
they made really unpleasant honey

33
00:01:45,550 --> 00:01:50,012
and they were also too big to pollinate many of our native flowers here

34
00:01:50,012 --> 00:01:50,650
in Australia.

35
00:01:50,650 --> 00:01:54,864
That must have had a devastating effect on the natural flora.

36
00:01:54,864 --> 00:01:56,780
Did you lose any species?

37
00:01:56,780 --> 00:02:00,720
No, we managed to get them under control before that happened,

38
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:04,660
but if Asian bees got in there could be other consequences.

39
00:02:04,660 --> 00:02:09,395
We could lose a lot of money because you might not be aware,

40
00:02:09,395 --> 00:02:14,495
but it's estimated that native bees' pollination of flower and vegetable crops is worth

41
00:02:14,495 --> 00:02:16,680
1.2 billion dollars a year.

42
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:19,312
So in a way they're the farmers' friend.

43
00:02:19,312 --> 00:02:19,641
Oh,

44
00:02:19,641 --> 00:02:23,590
and another thing is, if you're stung by an Asian Honey Bee,

45
00:02:23,590 --> 00:02:26,481
it can produce an allergic reaction in some people,

46
00:02:26,481 --> 00:02:29,050
so they're much more dangerous than native bees.

47
00:02:29,050 --> 00:02:32,750
How will you know if Asian bees have entered Australia?

48
00:02:32,750 --> 00:02:37,220
We're looking at the diet of the bird called the Rainbow Bee Eater.

49
00:02:37,220 --> 00:02:41,660
The Bee Eater doesn't care what it eats, as long as they're insects.

50
00:02:41,660 --> 00:02:47,606
But the interesting thing about this bird is that we are able to analyse exactly what it eats

51
00:02:47,606 --> 00:02:50,909
and that's really helpful if we're looking for introduced insects.

52
00:02:50,909 --> 00:02:51,570
How come?

53
00:02:51,570 --> 00:02:54,761
Because insects have their skeletons outside their bodies,

54
00:02:54,761 --> 00:02:58,750
so the Bee Eaters digest the meat from the inside.

55
00:02:58,750 --> 00:03:02,439
Then they bring up all the indigestible bits of skeleton

56
00:03:02,439 --> 00:03:07,235
and, of course, the wings in a pellet - a small ball of waste

57
00:03:07,235 --> 00:03:09,080
material which they cough up.

58
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:10,492
That sounds a bit unpleasant.

59
00:03:10,492 --> 00:03:12,470
So, how do you go about it?

60
00:03:12,470 --> 00:03:16,880
In the field we track down the Bee Eaters and find their favourite feeding

61
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:20,030
spots, you know, the places where the birds usually feed.

62
00:03:20,030 --> 00:03:22,740
It's here that we can find the pellets.

63
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:27,481
We collect them up and take them back to the laboratory to examine the

64
00:03:27,481 --> 00:03:27,820
contents.

65
00:03:27,820 --> 00:03:29,218
How do you do that?

66
00:03:29,218 --> 00:03:33,132
The pellets are really hard, especially if they have been out in the sun

67
00:03:33,132 --> 00:03:34,250
for a few days

68
00:03:34,250 --> 00:03:39,128
so first of all, we treat them by adding water to moisten them

69
00:03:39,128 --> 00:03:40,628
and make them softer.

70
00:03:40,628 --> 00:03:43,630
Then we pull them apart under the microscope.

71
00:03:43,630 --> 00:03:49,441
Everything's all scrunched up but we're looking for wings so we just pull them all out

72
00:03:49,441 --> 00:03:50,530
and straighten them.

73
00:03:50,530 --> 00:03:54,681
Then we identify them to see if we can find any Asian bee wings.

74
00:03:54,681 --> 00:03:56,460
And how many have you found?

75
00:03:56,460 --> 00:04:01,631
So far our research shows that Asian bees have not entered Australia in any

76
00:04:01,631 --> 00:04:02,000
number -

77
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,716
it's a good result and much more reliable than trying to find live ones

78
00:04:06,716 --> 00:04:08,400
as evidence of introduced insects.

79
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:08,822
Well,

80
00:04:08,822 --> 00:04:09,667
that's fascinating!

81
00:04:09,667 --> 00:04:12,200
Thank you, Grant, for those insights.

82
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:16,698
I hope that you might inspire some of our students here to conduct some

83
00:04:16,698 --> 00:04:17,340
similar experiments.
